[size=5][b]popctl: population control for GTA IV: The Complete Edition[/b][/size]
[i]A research release. Source, figures, trace logs and issues: [url=https://github.com/gutbash/popctl]github.com/gutbash/popctl[/url][/i]

[size=4][b]Explainer[/b][/size]
[youtube]82yKo4NCL_g[/youtube]

[size=4][b]Abstract[/b][/size]
Ambient pedestrians in GTA IV vanish in groups at what looks like a fixed distance in front of the player, and no density setting changes it. The distance is not a constant. Pedestrians beyond roughly 30 m are lightweight dummy peds in a pool of 150 slots, and the population manager, whenever fewer than twelve slots are free, deletes the farthest entries at up to twenty per frame with no visibility test. On any busy street the pool is within twelve of full, so the crowd ends wherever the 138th-farthest pedestrian stands: a median 74 m on the test route, in batches, on camera. popctl 0.2 replaces that cull with one of the same budget that only takes pedestrians the camera cannot see, grows the pool to 300 together with the twelve engine pools sized to it, and moves the shared keep and spawn distances to 130 m. Every claim here comes from a hook on the one function all 49 removal paths pass through, which ships in the plugin as an option.

[size=4][b]Problem[/b][/size]
The community-standard levers for crowd density are popcycle.dat, the density multiplier natives and the vehicle density command-line flags. Tested against a pedestrian census on 2026-09-06, none of them moved the count past roughly 60 m.

popctl 0.1 then located a distance cull in the population manager at RVA 0x73AF50, comparing squared distance against 6400 (80 m) or 225 (15 m), and moved both. It shipped with the claim that this was the mechanism. Users reported no visible change and that pedestrians still vanished in groups. Both reports were correct: that loop skips every pedestrian the camera can see, so raising its distances changed what survived behind the player and nothing else. In a three-minute run it accounted for 25 deletions out of 9,485.

[size=4][b]Method[/b][/size]
[b]Trace.[/b] Every population cull ends in RemovePed at RVA 0x73BCD0. popctl hooks it (TraceRemovals = 1) and writes one line per deletion: the return address and two above it, the pool the entity came from, its distance from the player, whether the camera could see it, the flags the engine consulted, and the fill of both pools. Three hands-free runs of the same 180 s camera route through northern Algonquin were traced (stock, 0.1, 0.2), plus several hours of play on Star Junction.

[b]Mechanism.[/b] The disassembly then explained each caller. Ambient pedestrians beyond about 30 m are dummy peds (entity type 6, 944 bytes, pool 0x14B6F10, 150 slots), swapped for a full CPed (pool 0x14B6F1C, 120 slots) as the player approaches and back as they leave. The population code walks both pools with parallel functions:
[list]
[*]0x738430, far pool nearly full: under 12 free slots, delete the farthest, up to 20 per frame, no visibility test.
[*]0x737240, band cull, both pools: past 115 m deleted even on camera (x1.5 for peds flagged seen); 70 to 115 m deleted if off camera and never flagged; 2.5 to 3.5 s grace timer. Plain floats at 0xC45994 and 0xC45990.
[*]0x73AF50 and 0x738620, off-camera culls: past 80 m if flagged seen, else 15 m, immediate. This is all 0.1 patched.
[*]0x73C640 and 0x73C8C0: conversion between the pools; the pedestrian is replaced in place.
[/list]
Every one of them skips a pedestrian whose mission flag is set.

[b]Cull replacement.[/b] 0x738430 is replaced by a function with the same contract and budget that walks the pool, sorts by distance, and skips any candidate the camera can see. If every far pedestrian is in view, nothing is deleted and spawning pauses, which the spawner already handles: it checks free slots before creating.

[b]Pool growth.[/b] The far pool's size is four immediates in its constructor at 0x1C41E0. Growing only that crashed within a minute, twice, and each dump named the next dependency: a record pool sized exactly 120 + 150 (0x1C3F70), then an animation blender from a named pool of 300. The game does not null-check either. Enumerating the executable's 46 named pools and two template constructors gave the full set: three pools at exactly 300 (CAnimBlender, CDummyTask, Event) and six animation pools at multiples of 300. popctl scales all of them by (120 + far) / 270, verifies every immediate against its stock value first, writes none if any one fails, and reads the live pool headers back after the game constructs them.

[b]Distances.[/b] The band floats are written directly. The off-camera constants are shared read-only pool entries, so the comiss displacements at 0x73B04B, 0x73B054 and 0x738700 are repointed at floats inside the plugin. The spawner's far edge (0xC45980, stock 105) is moved to match.

[size=4][b]Findings[/b][/size]
Deletions in 180 s on the same route, every one logged:
[code]                          stock     popctl 0.1    popctl 0.2
far pool size             150       150           300
far pool fill, median     135       135           288
deletions, all paths      8,129     9,485         11,031
"pool nearly full"        4,426     6,192         7,503
  of those on camera      2,847     4,094         0
  median distance         74 m      74 m          n/a
the cull 0.1 patched      21        25            0
average frame rate        50.1      53.2          50.3[/code]
[list]
[*][b]The visible cut-off is pool pressure, not a distance constant.[/b] The far pool sat within twelve of full for the whole route in every configuration. That is why it looks like a fixed radius, why it happens in groups, and why no distance setting moved it.
[*][b]The spawner fills whatever it is given.[/b] At 150, 300 and 450 slots the pool was full within seconds. Capacity sets density; 0.1's doubled spawn rate only churned the pool faster and fired the batch cull more often. 0.2 returns it to stock.
[*][b]450 far peds broke physics on the test machine.[/b] With the pool at 438 of 450, peds and the player jittered vertically and fell through the map, which is what a starved frame budget does to the collision step. 300 ran clean for a full session and is the shipped value.
[*][b]Distance settings above the spawn band do nothing you can see.[/b] Once the batch cull no longer takes visible peds, the crowd's front edge is where the spawner stops placing them, 105 m in stock. 130 and 160 m looked identical until the spawn band moved with them.
[*][b]Conversions are not despawns.[/b] The two largest remaining callers replace a far ped with a full ped at about 30 m and back. The replacement stands where the original did.
[*]Frame rate on the route was within run-to-run noise across the three configurations. One route, one machine; not a performance characterisation.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Have an ASI loader present. Ultimate ASI Loader as dinput8.dll is the usual one, and if you run FusionFix you already have it.
[*]Put popctl.asi and popctl.ini in your GTAIV folder, next to GTAIV.exe.
[*]Launch. popctl.log records every patch attempt and its outcome.
[/list]
To uninstall, delete both files. Nothing is written to disk by the plugin and no game file is modified. Upgrading from 0.1: replace both files; the old FarKeepMetres key is still read.

[size=4][b]Verifying[/b][/size]
popctl.log is the source of truth. These lines mean it worked:
[code]pools: far pool 150 -> 300
pools: ped records 270 -> 420
patched: visible 130 m, hidden 130 m, near 130 m
pool cull: 0x738430 replaced; off-camera far peds go first, on-camera ones are never taken
pools: peds 120 x 3824 bytes, far peds 300 x 944 bytes, ped records 420 x 400 bytes[/code]
If any is missing, the log names the check that failed. TraceRemovals = 1 then shows you every deletion the game makes, with the path that made it.

[size=4][b]Limitations[/b][/size]
These are the boundaries of what was tested. Nothing outside them should be assumed to work.
[list]
[*][b]Complete Edition 1.2.0.59 only.[/b] Every address is a hardcoded offset for that exact build. On any other version the checks fail, nothing is patched, and the log says so.
[*][b]The full-ped pool is not grown.[/b] The 120 real peds within about 30 m peaked at 111 during play. Density close to the player does not change. Its dependents were not enumerated.
[*][b]Behind you, the crowd thins with distance.[/b] The pool is finite; when it is full the farthest off-camera pedestrians go first. Every view being ready needs more slots than the frame budget allowed.
[*][b]The vehicle pool cannot be grown this way.[/b] 140 to 200 with the car budget at 150 crashed after three minutes with the CPU jumping into a data address, the signature of a 140-entry table indexed by vehicle slot. The dump is in the repository. Traffic stays bounded by the 110-car budget.
[*][b]Interiors are unaffected.[/b] Subway stations and other interiors are populated by scenario points and the popcycle interior column, neither touched.
[*][b]Texture streaming is out of scope.[/b] Far peds past roughly 100 m draw untextured. Stock deleted them before you could see it. Lower VisibleKeepMetres if that bothers you more than crowd depth.
[*][b]One machine, one mod stack.[/b] A camera flythrough for the numbers, play on Star Junction for the rest, FusionFix loaded.
[*][b]The "seen" flag is inferred[/b] from how the engine uses it.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Reproducibility[/b][/size]
Turn on TraceRemovals, play, and read popctl_trace.log; docs/make_figures.py in the repository charts any trace. Anyone reproducing the numbers above should report game build, the route or play pattern, and the pool sizes from popctl.log. The open questions are the frame-time cost curve against FarPedPool, the full-ped pool's dependents, which table the vehicle pool overran, and the flag semantics.

[size=4][b]Verifying what you downloaded[/b][/size]
The full source is on GitHub under MIT, and every binary is built from that source by public GitHub Actions, so you can read the build log or build it yourself rather than taking the upload on trust. It is a single translation unit with no dependencies beyond the Win32 SDK.

popctl.asi SHA-256 2afd688dacd161810866c377ee39805c1562ec6bac519997eb73a47225989ecd

[size=4][b]Companion release[/b][/size]
[url=https://github.com/gutbash/revd]revd[/url] lifts GTA IV's twenty-five slot engine audio ceiling, which is what silences most cars in dense traffic. Separate limit, separate fix, independent of this one.

